  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0011 database contains nine tables, each with its own unique purpose. The first table, ticketit_statuses, stores information about the statuses of tickets in the system. It has three columns: id, name, and color. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the name and color columns are also not nullable. The second table, ticketit_priorities, stores information about the priorities of tickets in the system. It has the same columns as the first table. The third table, ticketit_categories, stores information about the categories of tickets in the system. It has the same columns as the first two tables. The fourth table, ticketit_categories_users, stores information about the categories of tickets that are assigned to specific users. It has two columns: category_id and user_id. The category_id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the user_id column is also not nullable. The fifth table, ticketit, stores information about the tickets themselves. It has 11 columns: id, subject, content, html, status_id, priority_id, user_id, agent_id, category_id, created_at, updated_at, and completed_at. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the subject, content, html, status_id, priority_id, user_id, agent_id, category_id, created_at, and updated_at columns are also not nullable. The sixth table, ticketit_comments, stores information about the comments made on tickets. It has six columns: id, user_id, ticket_id, created_at, updated_at, and content. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the user_id, ticket_id, created_at, updated_at, and content columns are also not nullable. The seventh table, ticketit_audits, stores information about the audit logs of tickets. It has six columns: id, operation, user_id, ticket_id, created_at, and updated_at. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the operation, user_id, ticket_id, created_at, and updated_at columns are also not nullable. The eighth table, users, stores information about the users of the system. It has three columns: id, ticketit_admin, and ticketit_agent. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the ticketit_admin and ticketit_agent columns are also not nullable. The ninth table, ticketit_settings, stores information about the settings of the system. It has seven columns: id, lang, slug, value, default, created_at, and updated_at. The id column is the primary key and is not nullable, while the lang, slug, value, default, created_at, and updated_at columns are also not nullable.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an open-source helpdesk ticketing system that seamlessly integrates with Laravel's default user authentication system. The solution supports Laravel versions 5.1 through 8. and can be easily integrated into existing Laravel projects. The helpdesk system aims to provide a simple and effective way to manage customer support tickets.\n\nObjectives\n\n1. To offer a straightforward integration of a helpdesk system within Laravel projects.\n2. To facilitate efficient ticket management for users, agents, and admins.\n3. To provide multilingual support using various language packs.\n4. To include analytics and performance tracking for administrative users.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n1. Users: Customers or team members who create and manage their support tickets.\n2. Agents: Staff responsible for handling and resolving tickets.\n3. Admins: Managers who oversee the ticketing process and system administration.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. User Roles:\n   - Users, agents, and admins have distinct roles with configurable access permissions.\n   \n2. Ticket Management:\n   - Users can create, track, comment on, and close their tickets.\n   - A text editor is available for ticket descriptions and comments, supporting image uploads.\n   \n3. Agent Assignment:\n   - The system automatically assigns tickets to agents based on departmental queues and agent load.\n   \n4. Localization:\n   - Support for multiple languages, including Arabic, Portuguese, German, English, Farsi, French, Hungarian, Italian, Persian, Russian, and Spanish.\n   \n5. Admin Dashboard:\n   - Features dashboards with statistics, graphical performance tracking, and simple administrative controls.\n\n6. Integration:\n   - Rapid installation and seamless integration with Laravel projects.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance:\n   - The system must efficiently manage ticket status and priority updates.\n   \n2. Usability:\n   - The interface should be user-friendly to ensure ease of use for all user roles.\n\n3. Scalability:\n   - The system should support a growing number of tickets and users without performance degradation.\n\n4. Security:\n   - Secure user authentication and data integrity must be maintained throughout ticket management.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0011.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0011\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_statuses` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_priorities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_categories` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_categories_users` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_categories_users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subject` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `html` text NOT NULL,\n  `status_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `priority_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `agent_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `completed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`status_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_statuses` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`priority_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_priorities` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit_categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_comments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_comments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_comments_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ticket_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_audits` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `operation` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_audits_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `ticketit_audits_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ticket_id`) REFERENCES `ticketit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ticketit_admin` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `ticketit_agent` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\nCREATE TABLE `ticketit_settings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `lang` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text NOT NULL,\n  `default` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
